WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.277
The most common bypass
is the endpoint itself,

2
00:00:03.277 --> 00:00:07.013
sending encrypted DNS straight
to a public resolver.

3
00:00:07.013 --> 00:00:09.768
Federal policy wants encrypted DNS,

4
00:00:09.768 --> 00:00:13.901
but terminated at agency
resolvers or Protective DNS.

5
00:00:13.901 --> 00:00:16.250
Each transport gives
you a different handle.

6
00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:18.837
Plain DNS can be redirected.

7
00:00:18.837 --> 00:00:24.412
DNS over TLS or QUIC can be blocked
except to approved resolvers.

8
00:00:24.412 --> 00:00:28.484
DNS over HTTPS hides inside
ordinary web traffic,

9
00:00:28.484 --> 00:00:31.463
so an edge-only design can't govern it.

10
00:00:31.463 --> 00:00:33.287
That's why the endpoint matters.

11
00:00:33.287 --> 00:00:37.396
On Windows, add the agency's
resolvers to the known DoH list,

12
00:00:37.396 --> 00:00:41.419
and note that since June 2026
the Windows Server 2025 DNS

13
00:00:41.419 --> 00:00:46.126
Server accepts client DoH, though
its upstream stays unencrypted.

14
00:00:46.126 --> 00:00:48.556
Browsers resolve names themselves,

15
00:00:48.556 --> 00:00:53.570
so the program adopts CISA's browser
configuration as mandatory policy.
